WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.720
Welcome back.

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.280
Today we turn our energy basics into a practical problem solving toolkit.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.840
We will connect changing forces, springs and collisions, and learn when energy

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.760
needs a second equation to help it.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.580
Start with a force that changes with position.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.520
Over a tiny displacement, the work is the force component along the motion

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:21.920
times that displacement.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.040
Adding these little contributions gives an integral.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.780
On a force versus position graph, work is the signed area under the curve.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.900
Do not confuse it with a force versus time graph, whose area gives impulse.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.620
Let the net force along a straight line be two x plus two newtons, where x is

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.140
measured in meters.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.883
A two kilogram particle starts from rest at x equal to zero and reaches x

00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:47.220
equal

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:47.660
to three.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.500
The force rises from two to eight newtons.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.900
Predict the work before doing calculus.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.180
The graph is a trapezoid, so its area is the average of two and eight,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:58.460
multiplied by three.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.380
That gives fifteen joules.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:06.029
Integrating gives x squared plus two x, evaluated from zero to three: nine

00:01:06.029 --> 00:01:06.280
plus

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.720
six, again fifteen.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.680
Net work equals the change in kinetic energy.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.960
For our two kilogram particle, one half m v squared is simply v squared in SI

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:16.260
units.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.400
The final speed is therefore the square root of fifteen meters per second.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.140
Negative areas subtract work; they do not become positive because we call them

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:25.520
areas.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.240
If the plotted force is only one of several forces, add the work of the others

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.360
before applying the theorem.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.920
Check the horizontal axis first: position gives energy, time gives momentum

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:37.400
change.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.240
Two motors can do the same work, yet finish at different times.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.260
Power measures the rate of energy transfer.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.840
Average power is work divided by the time interval.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.400
Instantaneous power is the dot product of force and velocity.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.574
In words, take the component of force along the velocity and multiply by

00:01:58.574 --> 00:01:58.940
speed.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.540
Why the dot product?

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.980
Work already counts the force component along displacement.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.060
Dividing a tiny amount of work by its tiny time interval replaces displacement

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:09.980
by velocity.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.780
Imagine lifting a four kilogram load vertically at a steady two meters per

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:15.000
second.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.380
Use g equal to ten and neglect other resistance.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.700
Steady velocity means zero acceleration, so the upward lifting force is forty

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.080
newtons.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.300
Its mechanical power is forty times two, or eighty watts.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.340
Gravity's power is minus eighty watts.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:36.340
The net power is zero, consistent with unchanged kinetic energy, while

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.420
gravitational potential energy increases.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.640
This is a nice reminder: zero net work on a particle does not mean the motor

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:44.380
does no work.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.000
Suppose the motor is eighty percent efficient.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.080
What electrical input power is needed for this eighty watt useful output?

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.340
Think about which number should be larger.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.560
Efficiency is useful output divided by input.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.620
Input is eighty divided by zero point eight, or one hundred watts.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.120
If a pulling force stays fixed while speed doubles along the same direction,

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.340
its instantaneous power doubles.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:12.860
If supplied power is fixed instead, the available force is power divided by

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.280
speed for collinear motion.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.540
Use this only where the constant power model applies; it does not predict a

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.620
realistic infinite force at zero speed.

00:03:21.816 --> 00:03:25.376
A spring becomes harder to stretch as its deformation grows.

00:03:26.276 --> 00:03:30.376
For an ideal spring within its linear elastic range, its force on the attached

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.956
block is minus k x.

00:03:32.956 --> 00:03:37.756
Here x is the signed displacement from the spring's natural length, and k is

00:03:37.756 --> 00:03:38.356
its stiffness.

00:03:39.096 --> 00:03:42.536
The minus sign says the spring force points back toward natural length.

00:03:43.396 --> 00:03:47.936
To store energy slowly, an external agent must oppose that changing force.

00:03:48.496 --> 00:03:50.956
The force versus extension graph is a triangle.

00:03:50.956 --> 00:03:55.776
Its area gives one half k x squared, the elastic potential energy.

00:03:56.956 --> 00:03:59.616
Compression stores energy too because x is squared.

00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:04.436
Take a two kilogram block attached to a light spring on a smooth horizontal

00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:04.836
surface.

00:04:05.616 --> 00:04:07.676
The stiffness is two hundred newtons per meter.

00:04:09.016 --> 00:04:12.516
Compress the spring by zero point two meters and release the block from rest.

00:04:13.116 --> 00:04:17.036
The stored energy is one half times two hundred times zero point two squared,

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:18.796
or four joules.

00:04:18.796 --> 00:04:21.036
At natural length the spring energy is zero.

00:04:21.616 --> 00:04:24.976
With no dissipative work, those four joules become kinetic energy.

00:04:25.776 --> 00:04:30.156
One half times two times v squared equals four, so the speed is two meters per

00:04:30.156 --> 00:04:30.356
second.

00:04:31.036 --> 00:04:34.496
Now add a rough region that removes one joule before the block reaches natural

00:04:34.496 --> 00:04:34.756
length.

00:04:35.636 --> 00:04:36.276
What changes?

00:04:36.996 --> 00:04:37.136
Pause.

00:04:37.736 --> 00:04:41.616
Only three joules remain as kinetic energy, so the speed becomes the square

00:04:41.616 --> 00:04:42.976
root of three meters per second.

00:04:43.756 --> 00:04:47.776
Do not call energy destroyed: it has transferred into internal energy of the

00:04:47.776 --> 00:04:48.616
contacting materials.

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:54.116
For this chosen system, initial mechanical energy plus nonconservative work

00:04:54.116 --> 00:04:56.016
equals final mechanical energy.

00:04:57.436 --> 00:05:00.816
State which forces are represented by potential energy so you do not count

00:05:00.816 --> 00:05:01.556
their work twice.

00:05:01.776 --> 00:05:04.836
Here is a problem where energy alone is not enough.

00:05:05.476 --> 00:05:09.216
A small particle on a light inextensible string moves in a vertical circle.

00:05:10.136 --> 00:05:12.356
Gravity is uniform and we neglect drag.

00:05:13.096 --> 00:05:16.856
The string must remain taut, and a string can pull but cannot push.

00:05:17.656 --> 00:05:19.736
At the top, inward means downward.

00:05:20.376 --> 00:05:25.916
Both weight and tension point inward, so T plus m g equals m v top squared

00:05:25.916 --> 00:05:26.976
divided by r.

00:05:27.436 --> 00:05:31.456
The smallest possible top speed for the marginal taut condition has tension

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:32.676
equal to zero.

00:05:32.956 --> 00:05:35.776
That gives v top squared equal to g r.

00:05:35.776 --> 00:05:38.396
A slightly larger speed gives positive tension.

00:05:39.296 --> 00:05:41.276
Now connect bottom and top using energy.

00:05:41.956 --> 00:05:46.196
The particle rises by two r, and ideal tension does no work because it is

00:05:46.196 --> 00:05:47.256
perpendicular to the motion.

00:05:48.016 --> 00:05:52.176
The decrease in kinetic energy equals m g times two r.

00:05:52.176 --> 00:05:55.996
Therefore v bottom squared equals v top squared plus four g r.

00:05:55.996 --> 00:06:00.116
Substitute the limiting top value and obtain five g r.

00:06:00.116 --> 00:06:04.196
The minimum bottom speed is the square root of five g r for this ideal string

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:04.536
model.

00:06:05.396 --> 00:06:08.196
Try r equal to two meters and g equal to ten.

00:06:08.816 --> 00:06:13.256
The minimum bottom speed is ten meters per second, while the top speed is the

00:06:13.256 --> 00:06:14.736
square root of twenty.

00:06:14.736 --> 00:06:19.388
For a one kilogram particle at the bottom, inward is upward: T minus ten

00:06:19.388 --> 00:06:19.776
equals

00:06:19.776 --> 00:06:21.556
one times one hundred divided by two.

00:06:22.296 --> 00:06:23.776
Bottom tension is sixty newtons.

00:06:23.976 --> 00:06:27.876
That tension is not forty; remember to include weight in the force balance.

00:06:28.656 --> 00:06:32.136
A rigid rod or a particle constrained on a track has different contact

00:06:32.136 --> 00:06:35.336
conditions, so do not transfer the string result automatically.

00:06:36.156 --> 00:06:40.316
In JEE, energy connects speeds at different heights, while the radial force

00:06:40.316 --> 00:06:43.356
equation checks whether the assumed motion is physically allowed.

00:06:43.584 --> 00:06:47.024
For a collision, begin by choosing both bodies as your system.

00:06:47.984 --> 00:06:51.984
During the short interaction, neglect net external impulse when the stated

00:06:51.984 --> 00:06:53.364
conditions justify it.

00:06:54.104 --> 00:06:56.084
Then total momentum is conserved.

00:06:56.964 --> 00:06:59.304
This does not automatically conserve kinetic energy.

00:07:00.024 --> 00:07:03.144
In an elastic collision, kinetic energy is also conserved.

00:07:03.624 --> 00:07:07.798
In a perfectly inelastic collision, the bodies stick together and share a

00:07:07.798 --> 00:07:08.084
final

00:07:08.084 --> 00:07:08.484
velocity.

00:07:09.124 --> 00:07:13.624
Let a two kilogram body moving right at six meters per second hit a stationary

00:07:13.624 --> 00:07:16.644
one kilogram body in a one dimensional elastic collision.

00:07:17.624 --> 00:07:21.444
Initial momentum is twelve kilogram meters per second, and kinetic energy is

00:07:21.444 --> 00:07:22.804
thirty six joules.

00:07:22.804 --> 00:07:25.624
The elastic separation speed equals the approach speed.

00:07:26.344 --> 00:07:31.584
So v two minus v one equals six, while momentum gives two v one plus v two

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:33.304
equals twelve.

00:07:33.304 --> 00:07:35.644
Substitute v two equal to v one plus six.

00:07:35.644 --> 00:07:41.364
Three v one equals six, giving v one equal to two and v two equal to eight

00:07:41.364 --> 00:07:42.144
meters per second.

00:07:43.024 --> 00:07:46.904
Check the energy: the two kilogram body has four joules and the one kilogram

00:07:46.904 --> 00:07:48.444
body has thirty two.

00:07:48.444 --> 00:07:50.184
Total, thirty six.

00:07:50.184 --> 00:07:52.264
Now let the same bodies stick together instead.

00:07:53.004 --> 00:07:56.824
Momentum gives three times their common velocity equal to twelve, so the

00:07:56.824 --> 00:07:57.744
velocity is four.

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:03.071
Final kinetic energy is one half times three times sixteen, or twenty four

00:08:03.071 --> 00:08:03.484
joules.

00:08:03.484 --> 00:08:08.704
The twelve joule decrease becomes other forms of energy; total energy has not

00:08:08.704 --> 00:08:09.084
vanished.

00:08:10.224 --> 00:08:13.864
Momentum is a signed equation, whereas kinetic energy uses speed squared.

00:08:14.644 --> 00:08:17.864
These examples assume translational particle motion without rotation.

00:08:18.684 --> 00:08:22.912
Do not impose both sticking and elastic kinetic energy conservation on a

00:08:22.912 --> 00:08:23.264
moving

00:08:23.264 --> 00:08:24.240
collision: those are different models.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.480
Momentum conservation is a vector statement, so a collision does not have to

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.360
stay on one line.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.080
Take a two kilogram particle traveling east at three meters per second and a

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.620
one kilogram particle traveling north at six meters per second.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.620
They stick together; external impulse is negligible, and treat the combined

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.200
object as a particle with no rotational energy.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.520
Choose east as positive x and north as positive y.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.780
Initial x momentum is six, and initial y momentum is also six.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.540
The combined mass is three kilograms.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:04.880
Divide each component by three, giving a final velocity of two east and two

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:06.480
north, in meters per second.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.340
The speed is the square root of two squared plus two squared, or two times the

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:11.980
square root of two.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.060
The direction is forty five degrees north of east.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.700
Why not add three and six to get nine?

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.320
Because the initial velocities belong to different masses and different

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:21.780
directions.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.240
Add momentum components, not speed magnitudes.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:29.800
Initial kinetic energy is nine plus eighteen, or twenty seven joules.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.740
Final kinetic energy is one half times three times eight, or twelve joules.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.960
Fifteen joules have left translational kinetic energy.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.100
Now consider equal masses in an elastic scattering example.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.920
One starts at ten meters per second east; the other is stationary.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:51.440
Possible final velocities are five east plus five north, and five east plus

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.040
five south.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.600
Their vector sum preserves momentum, and their equal speeds of five times

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.700
square root of two preserve the initial fifty joules for one kilogram masses.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.480
The final velocity vectors are perpendicular.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:08.540
This special result excludes a zero final velocity and assumes an initially

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.040
stationary equal mass target.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.180
General two dimensional scattering needs enough angle or interaction

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.380
information to determine a unique outcome.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:20.540
Use the practice questions to test the model before trusting a memorized

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:20.820
formula.
